Lake Garda and northern Italy covers  some 370 square kilometres and is  surrounded by Alpine foothills the  northern shore boasts an enticing mix of  rugged natural beauty and picturesque  villages one of the best known is Lamoni  sugata until the late 1920s this was a  fishing village and could only be  reached by boat from Coos Rd has lived  in Lamoni for over 70 years he witnessed  the first tourist boom quando mussolini  queen tourism began here in 1932 when  Mussolini ordered the construction of  the goddess on a lakeside Road in the  1950s hotels replaced entire lemon  groves here with a diamo  county hotel it was at that time that  Lamoni Sol Garda with its narrow streets  and romantic piazzas became a favorite  vacation destination for many Europeans  especially Germans  a small Museum reminds visitors of those  days the progressive Northern European  female tourists were something of a  revelation to local young people they  don't know most of you they were much  more forward and open than we were we  boys would line up to go out with them  dance with them and get to know them and  in doing so we contributed to the  development of tourism here when the  holiday was over the women went home and  raved about us young Italian men  okano Sciuto they called us Latin lovers  Johnny is latin lover man limo Nestle  Garda is said to be the northernmost  point on earth where lemons girl  tourists can still see a few of the  remaining lemon groves these days it's  not just Latin lovers who draw visitors  to Lake gardez northern shore the winds  here make the spot a paradise for  surfers and sailors on the other side of  the lake a cable car takes visitors up  Monte Baldo each year around half a  million people visit this 30 kilometer  long mountain ridge on a good day you  can see Venice from here or take  advantage of the winds and go  paragliding for a bird's-eye view
 

